    "innovation": "This paper analyzes long-term ice loss for individual Antarctic drainage basins, identifying distinct critical temperature thresholds and demonstrating that the Antarctic Ice Sheet acts as several interacting tipping systems rather than a single monolithic tipping element.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Parallel Ice Sheet Model (PISM)",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "hybrid shallow ice flow model",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "PISM is a hybrid shallow ice flow model in which ice flow velocities resulting from two stress balance approximations-the shallow ice and shallow shelf approximations-are superimposed over the entire ice-sheet/ice-shelf domain. This ensures smooth and consistent transitions between the different flow regimes in the interior of the ice sheet, where flow is dominated by vertical shearing, in the ice streams, which are sliding on the bed and in the floating ice shelves, where flow is characterized by fast plug flow.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Parallel Ice Sheet Model (PISM)",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "16-km horizontal resolution",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "The simulations are performed using a regular rectangular grid with 16-km horizontal resolution. The vertical grid spacing is quadratic, ranging from 20 m at the ice base to 100 m at the top of the computational domain. While computational feasibility of our long-term simulations puts a strong limit on model grid resolution, PISM uses a subgrid-scale linear interpolation of the basal shear stress between adjacent grounded and floating cells to ensure a free and reversible grounding-line migration, which has been shown to com- pare well with full-Stokes results throughout a wide range of spatial resolutions-including the resolution used here-qualitatively as well as quantitatively 98.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Parallel Ice Sheet Model (PISM)",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "generalized pseudoplastic power law",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "The sliding of the ice sheet over the underlying bedrock is represented by a generalized pseudoplastic power law, which relates bed-parallel shear stress \u03c4b and basal sliding velocity ub (ref. 99): \u03c4b=-\u03c4c(ub/u\u2080)\u00b9/q where q = 0.75 is the pseudoplastic sliding exponent and u\u2080 = 100 m yr\u00af\u00b9 is a reference velocity.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": false,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Parallel Ice Sheet Model (PISM)",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Mohr-Coulomb criterion",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "The yield stress \u03c4c is determined according to the Mohr-Coulomb criterion based on the till friction angle (an heuristic shear strength parameter for the till material property) and the effective till pressure 100.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Parallel Ice Sheet Model (PISM)",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "eigencalving",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Iceberg calving at the margins of the ice shelves is calculated on the basis of spreading rates ('eigencalving', ref. 101), using a proportionality factor of 1017 m s. To ensure numerical stability, we further apply a minimum thickness criterion of 50 m at the calving front102 and remove floating ice in cells which were marked as open ocean during the initial timestep.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Parallel Ice Sheet Model (PISM)",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "viscoelastic Earth-deformation model",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "The glacial isostatic adjustment of the bedrock and seafloor in response to changing ice loads are modelled using a viscoelastic Earth-deformation model103,104, assuming a spatially uniform upper mantle viscosity of 1021 Pa s (ref. 68) and a standard density of 3,300 kg m\u00b3. As we here only consider equilibrium ice-sheet states, we find only little sensitivity of our results to variations in Earth model parameters. Lower mantle viscosities and lower flexural rigidity (thinner elastic lithosphere), however, can slow transient ice-sheet retreat and shift thresholds to slightly higher (<1 \u00b0C) temperatures (Extended Data Fig. 4a,b).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Parallel Ice Sheet Model (PISM)",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "positive degree-day (PDD) scheme",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "At the ice-atmosphere boundary, we compute melt and runoff using a positive degree-day (PDD) scheme with melt coefficients of 3 mm PDD\u00af\u00b9 and 8 mm PDD\u00af\u00b9 for snow and ice, respectively, and a 5 \u00b0C standard deviation to account for diurnal cycles and synoptic variability, applying a sinusoidal yearly temperature cycle 105,106.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Parallel Ice Sheet Model (PISM)",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Potsdam Ice-shelf Cavity Model (PICO)",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Subshelf melting at the ice-ocean boundary underneath the ice shelves is computed using the Potsdam Ice-shelf Cavity Model (PICO; ref. 109). We drive PICO with observed ocean temperature and salinity data from ref. 110, averaged over the period 1975-2012. For the overturning strength coefficient and turbulent heat exchange velocity across the ice-ocean boundary we adopt parameter values of 0.5 Sv (kg m\u00b3)-1 and 10-5 m s\u00af\u00b9, respectively. These values are the same as in refs. 76,111 and slightly lower than the values used in refs. 60,109, resulting in a slightly conservative estimate of subshelf melt rates.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Parallel Ice Sheet Model (PISM)",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "root-mean-square error",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "Extended Data Fig. 1 | Comparison of modelled and observed ice geometry. a, Observed Antarctic ice surface elevation from the Bedmap2 dataset (ref. 95), regridded to 16 km. Grounding lines are shown in white. b, Modelled ice surface elevation of the reference equilibrium state serving as initial configuration for the experiments. Grounding lines are shown in white. c, Difference between modelled and observed ice surface elevations. d, Scatter plot comparing modelled and observed ice thickness for each model grid cell. The grey line illustrates where modelled ice thickness would perfectly match the observations. RMSE, root-mean-square error.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
    ],
